Installing DuckDB from this packaging fork
==========================================

This page is for end users who want a `.deb` installation of
DuckDB on Debian or Ubuntu.  Packaging maintainers and OBS
publishing are documented separately in debian/README.source and
debian/README.OBS.

Available channels
------------------

  1. GitHub Pages apt repository (recommended — one-line apt install)
     See debian/README.apt-pages.  Debian + Ubuntu, amd64 + arm64.
  2. GitHub Release downloads (manual .deb download, no apt update).
  3. Launchpad PPA (Ubuntu only).  See debian/README.PPA.
  4. openSUSE Build Service apt repository (planned; pending OBS
     account activation).  See debian/README.OBS.

All four produce the same three binary packages built from the same
source:

  libduckdb1      shared library
  libduckdb-dev   headers + .so symlink + CMake config + example
  duckdb          command-line shell

1. GitHub Pages apt repository (recommended)
--------------------------------------------

The packaging fork ships its own apt repository at
https://mgajda.github.io/duckdb-deb/ — one-line `apt install duckdb`
once you've added the source.

Three-step install (replace SUITE with trixie / sid / jammy / noble
to match your distribution):

    sudo install -d -m 0755 /etc/apt/keyrings
    curl -fsSL https://mgajda.github.io/duckdb-deb/duckdb-deb-archive-key.asc \
        | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/duckdb-deb.gpg

    echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/duckdb-deb.gpg] \
          https://mgajda.github.io/duckdb-deb SUITE main" \
        | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/duckdb-deb.list

    sudo apt-get update
    sudo apt-get install duckdb libduckdb-dev

This channel signs every Release with the maintainer's GPG key
(verifiable at the URL above), gets automatic upgrades via
`apt update`, and supports both amd64 and arm64.  Full instructions
including key-rotation procedure are in debian/README.apt-pages.

2. GitHub Release downloads
---------------------------

For users who prefer not to add a third-party apt source, every
tagged release (v*) attaches its full build matrix to the
GitHub Release page.  Find the matching `.deb` for your
distribution at:

    https://github.com/mgajda/duckdb-deb/releases

The filename includes the distribution codename and architecture,
e.g.:

    libduckdb1_1.5.2+git20260517.c4e422bbe4-1_amd64.deb   (Debian trixie)
    duckdb_1.5.2+git20260517.c4e422bbe4-1_amd64.deb

For untagged development snapshots, builds also appear as workflow
artefacts under
https://github.com/mgajda/duckdb-deb/actions/workflows/debian-package.yml
— but those are retained for 30 days only.  Use a tagged release if
you need a stable URL.

To install, download all three .deb files for your distribution and:

    sudo apt install ./libduckdb1_*.deb ./libduckdb-dev_*.deb ./duckdb_*.deb

`apt` (rather than `dpkg -i`) resolves the inter-package
dependencies for you.

Upgrades are manual: re-download the new release's .debs and
`sudo apt install ./*.deb` again.  This is the trade-off of the
"download from a release page" model.  For automatic upgrades via
`apt update`, use channel 1 above.

Verifying downloads
-------------------

The .deb files served from a GH Release are not GPG-signed by an
individual key.  GitHub serves them over HTTPS from
github-releases.githubusercontent.com, which gives you
transport-level integrity but not author authentication beyond
"whoever owns the github.com/mgajda account uploaded this".

If you need stronger guarantees, use channel 1 (apt repo with
signed Release files), or build from source: clone
github.com/mgajda/duckdb-deb at the matching tag and run
`dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b` yourself.  See debian/README.source
for the build prerequisites.

3. Launchpad PPA (planned)
--------------------------

Once active, install via:

    sudo add-apt-repository ppa:mgajda/duckdb-deb
    sudo apt-get update
    sudo apt-get install duckdb libduckdb-dev

Ubuntu only (Launchpad-side limitation; Debian users should use
channel 1).  See debian/README.PPA for the maintainer's
publishing workflow and the current status of PPA activation.

4. OBS apt repository (planned)
-------------------------------

Once the OBS account is active, this section will become:

    echo 'deb https://download.opensuse.org/repositories/home:/mjgajda/Debian_13/ /' \
        | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/mjgajda-duckdb.list
    curl -fsSL https://download.opensuse.org/repositories/home:/mjgajda/Debian_13/Release.key \
        | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/mjgajda-duckdb.gpg
    sudo apt update
    sudo apt install duckdb

with one entry per supported target (Debian_13, Debian_Unstable,
xUbuntu_22.04, xUbuntu_24.04).  Until then, use channel 1 or 2.

Supported distributions
-----------------------

  Debian 13 (trixie)        amd64, arm64
  Debian unstable (sid)     amd64, arm64
  Ubuntu 22.04 (jammy)      amd64, arm64
  Ubuntu 24.04 (noble)      amd64, arm64

riscv64 is built under qemu-user emulation in CI as a forward-
looking signal (see debian-sid-riscv64 in the workflow) but is
NOT yet promoted to the apt repo — there is no native riscv64
runner to autopkgtest against, so the .debs are not installation-
verified.

Older Debian releases (bookworm, bullseye) are not targeted: the
package requires g++ (>= 11) and CMake (>= 3.14), which are not
both available in those releases without backports.

Reporting bugs
--------------

Packaging bugs: https://github.com/mgajda/duckdb-deb/issues
DuckDB itself: https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/issues
